    <div>
      <p><strong>The Cummins Home</strong></p>
      <p><strong>401 2nd Ave NW</strong></p>

      <p>
        This home, presently owned by Nancy Pfenning, was originally built by
        Theodore A. Cummins. It is believed that this home was built in 1888
        while Mr. Cummins was employed with the Northern Pacific Railroad. It
        is one of many homes in this area that were built on railroad property
        that was broke down and sold off into sections.
      </p>

      <p>
        Theodore came to Mandan from Davenport, Iowa at the age of 19 when he
        was actually on his way to Glendive, Montana to find employment with
        the railroad. In Mandan, he worked for the railroad as a timekeeper,
        chief clerk, and as the first store keeper. His parents later joined
        him and engaged in the construction business.
      </p>

      <p>
        Three years after his arrival and while still employed with the
        railroad, Theodore formed a partnership with a Mr. Granberry in the
        clothing business. When Mr. Granberry passed away four years later,
        Theodore left his employment at the railroad and formed a partnership
        with W. C. Tubbs in the clothing store. Later another partner, Mr.
        Thorberg, was taken on. The clothing store continued to change
        partners, but Theodore always remained a primary shareholder.
        Eventually the business was taken over by Theodore’s sons T.A. and
        Robert. The Cummins Clothier was operated until 1959 when it announced
        its closeout sale, which signaled the finale of Mandan’s oldest
        clothing store.
      </p>

      <p>
        Theodore was not only a businessman, but also a family man. He shared
        his life with his beautiful wife Margaret, two daughters, and two
        sons. Theodore also stayed active in community affairs as director of
        the First National Bank, President of the Mandan Rodeo Association,
        and commander of the American Legion Post. He also had affiliations
        with the Rotary Club and interests in other civic matters.
      </p>

      <p>
        The home is an exceptional example of homes built during this era. The
        workmanship and craftsmanship show how well built homes were during
        early years. They needed to be durable for the harsh winters, but also
        make a statement as to one’s social station.
      </p>

      <p class="source-credit">Draft transcription from the photographed sign; it has not yet received a second review.</p>
